[ ] F3 (changes) `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_anticommuting-probability.tex:62` — The `d = 0` counterexample is outside the paper's positive-integer parameter domain and does not show a missing source hypothesis.  <!-- lane=code -->\\\\n- [ ] F4 (blocker) `blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:1767` — The new `\\\\\\\\leanok` certifies a directly indexed, option-completed theorem as though only the source error term changed.  <!-- lane=prose -->\\\\n- [ ] F5 (changes) `blueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:750` — The newly certified proof sketch and `\\\\\\\\uses` list do not describe the Lean proof.  <!-- lane=prose -->\\\\n- [ ] F6 (changes) `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt-gap-register.md:17` — The register incorrectly says both extended-lines declarations match their source or blueprint statements.  <!-- lane=prose -->\\\\n- [ ] F7 (changes) `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt-gap-register.md:22` — The register says the blueprint uses the directly indexed line space, although its source-labelled nodes retain the seed-indexed distribution.  <!-- lane=prose -->\\\\n- [ ] F8 (changes) `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_combined-lines-error-term.tex:295` — The note incorrectly calls the direct-line declaration source-shaped without recording its carrier and outcome changes.  <!-- lane=prose -->\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr184-20260905-04.jsonl-337-{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_172\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/bash -lc \\\\\\\"nl -ba docs/mathematical_language.md | sed -n '1,260p'\\\\nnl -ba docs/blueprint_style_guide.md | sed -n '1,210p'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr184-20260905-04.jsonl-338-{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_172\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/bash -lc \\\\\\\"nl -ba docs/mathematical_language.md | sed -n '1,260p'\\\\nnl -ba docs/blueprint_style_guide.md | sed -n '1,210p'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"     1\\\\t# Mathematical Language Guidelines\\\\n     2\\\\t\\\\n     3\\\\tMIPStarRE Lean names and documentation should be written for readers comparing\\\\n     4\\\\tthe formalization with the paper and blueprint.\\\\n     5\\\\t\\\\n     6\\\\tThis is a project-local supplement to the Mathlib-derived documentation,\\\\n     7\\\\tnaming, and style guides in the lean-conventions skill's `MATHLIB_doc`,\\\\n     8\\\\t`MATHLIB_naming`, and `MATHLIB_style` references.\\\\n     9\\\\t\\\\n    10\\\\t## Scope\\\\n    11\\\\t\\\\n    12\\\\tThese guidelines apply to:\\\\n    13\\\\t\\\\n    14\\\\t- public Lean declaration names and namespace names;\\\\n    15\\\\t- module docstrings;\\\\n    16\\\\t- declaration docstrings;\\\\n    17\\\\t- documentation-visible section comments;\\\\n    18\\\\t- explanatory comments that describe the mathematical role of a declaration.\\\\n    19\\\\t- blueprint prose and formalization-support notes;\\\\n    20\\\\t- issue titles and issue bodies;\\\\n    21\\\\t- PR titles and PR descriptions.\\\\n    22\\\\t\\\\n    23\\\\t## Source of Truth\\\\n    24\\\\t\\\\n    25\\\\tUse terminology from the following sources, in this order:\\\\n    26\\\\t\\\\n    27\\\\t1. `references/ldt-paper/`\\\\n    28\\\\t2. `blueprint/src/chapter/`\\\\n    29\\\\t3. established local conventions in nearby Lean files\\\\n    30\\\\t\\\\n    31\\\\tWhen the paper has a term for the object, use that term. For Section 5, for\\\\n    32\\\\texample, prefer orthogonalization lemma, truncation function, rounding to\\\\n    33\\\\tprojectors, rank reduction, and completing to measurement.\\\\n    34\\\\t\\\\n    35\\\\t## Reference Notation And Terminology\\\\n    36\\\\t\\\\n    37\\\\tThe notation and terminology of the original reference are the default for\\\\n    38\\\\treader-facing mathematical text.  A reader should be able to compare a Lean\\\\n    39\\\\tdocstring, blueprint paragraph, issue, or PR description with the cited source\\\\n    40\\\\twithout first learning a private vocabulary used by the formalization.\\\\n    41\\\\t\\\\n    42\\\\tWhen Lean must use a different name or representation, define the deviation in a\\\\n    43\\\\tlocation which future readers can find from the affected statement.  Use the\\\\n    44\\\\tfirst applicable location in the following order:\\\\n    45\\\\t\\\\n    46\\\\t1. the declaration docstring of the public object whose name or representation\\\\n    47\\\\t   differs from the paper;\\\\n    48\\\\t2. the module docstring of the defining Lean file, when the deviation concerns a\\\\n    49\\\\t   family of declarations in that file;\\\\n    50\\\\t3. the relevant blueprint paragraph, when the notation appears only in the\\\\n    51\\\\t   blueprint;\\\\n    52\\\\t4. a report under `docs/reports/` or `docs/paper-gaps/`, when the deviation is a\\\\n    53\\\\t   larger discrepancy between the paper, blueprint, and Lean.\\\\n    54\\\\t\\\\n    55\\\\tThe explanation should say:\\\\n    56\\\\t\\\\n    57\\\\t- the notation or term used in the paper;\\\\n    58\\\\t- the Lean object or name which represents it;\\\\n    59\\\\t- the mathematical reason for the difference, if there is one;\\\\n    60\\\\t- the scope in which the replacement should be used.\\\\n    61\\\\t\\\\n    62\\\\tA PR description may mention the deviation, but it should not be the only place\\\\n    63\\\\twhere future readers can learn it.\\\\n    64\\\\t\\\\n    65\\\\tFormalization-only auxiliary lemmas should be introduced as such.  Their prose\\\\n    66\\\\tshould name the nearby paper equation, theorem, or construction they support,\\\\n    67\\\\trather than presenting the auxiliary name as paper terminology.\\\\n    68\\\\t\\\\n    69\\\\t## Naming Norm\\\\n    70\\\\t\\\\n    71\\\\tNames and prose should describe the mathematical object, hypothesis, or\\\\n    72\\\\tconclusion. They should not encode the history of how the formalization was\\\\n    73\\\\tassembled.\\\\n    74\\\\t\\\\n    75\\\\tAvoid presenting words such as `pipeline`, `wrapper`, `package`, `raw`,\\\\n    76\\\\t`oneShot`, or `liveBlock` as mathematical terminology unless the cited source\\\\n    77\\\\tuses them with that meaning. Likewise, avoid implementation-local substitutes\\\\n    78\\\\tsuch as `projectivization` or `spectral truncation` when the paper gives a\\\\n    79\\\\tclearer phrase.\\\\n    80\\\\t\\\\n    81\\\\tThis is a general rule for the repository, not only a rule for file splits.\\\\n    82\\\\tModule names, namespace names, public declarations, theorem fields, docstrings,\\\\n    83\\\\tblueprint-facing names, issue titles, and spec documents should name the\\\\n    84\\\\tmathematical layer under discussion, not the local workflow step, paper line\\\\n    85\\\\tnumber, or implementation history.\\\\n    86\\\\t\\\\n    87\\\\t### Concrete examples\\\\n    88\\\\t\\\\n    89\\\\tThe table below illustrates the naming norm with examples patterned on the\\\\n    90\\\\tcodebase and on names seen during review.  Some entries are existing names;\\\\n    91\\\\tothers are proposed names for future refactors.  The \\\\\\\"avoid\\\\\\\" column lists\\\\n    92\\\\tprocess-shaped names that encode the history of how the formalization was\\\\n    93\\\\tassembled; the \\\\\\\"prefer\\\\\\\" column lists mathematical names that a reader of the\\\\n    94\\\\tpaper or blueprint would recognize.\\\\n    95\\\\t\\\\n    96\\\\t| Avoid (process-shaped)                        | Prefer (mathematical)                     | Rationale                                              |\\\\n    97\\\\t|:----------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n    98\\\\t| `Line130`                                     | `ProjectiveConsistency`                   | Paper Lemma 5.13, not \\\\\\\"the line-130 identity\\\\\\\"          |\\\\n    99\\\\t| `Line169`                                     | `CompletionTransport`                     | Paper Lemma 5.16, step that transports completed data  |\\\\n   100\\\\t| `Step6`                                       | `Orthonormalization`                      | Paper Section 5, the orthonormalization construction   |\\\\n   101\\\\t| `FinalAssembly`                               | `DiagonalCompletion`                      | Paper Lemma 5.17, constructing a diagonal POVM         |\\\\n   102\\\\t| `OneShotIneq`                                 | `SelfConsistencyDataProcessing` (or existing module `Preliminaries.SelfConsistency.DataProcessing`)           | Paper's data-processing inequality for self-consistency |\\\\n   103\\\\t| `PipelineCheck`                               | `ErrorPropagationBound` (proposed)                   | The bound that propagates error through the test       |\\\\n   104\\\\t| `QxpLayerWrapper`                             | `ProjectivePOVMCompletion` (proposed)                | The construction from round-projectors to POVMs        |\\\\n   105\\\\t| `RawSpectralTruncation`                       | `SpectralTruncation`                      | The paper's spectral truncation, not an \\\\\\\"unwrapped\\\\\\\" version |\\\\n   106\\\\t| `AddInUStep3To5`                              | `AddInUUpperExcessMass` (proposed)                  | Paper Addendum in §U, the upper excess-mass estimate   |\\\\n   107\\\\t| `AddInUStep12`                                | `AddInUCommutationTail` (proposed)                   | Paper Addendum in §U, the commutation tail estimate    |\\\\n   108\\\\t\\\\n   109\\\\tAn avoided name is acceptable only when the cited paper itself uses that\\\\n   110\\\\tphrase as the mathematical name (e.g., a lemma the paper calls \\\\\\\"Step 2\\\\\\\").\\\\n   111\\\\tIn that case the docstring should cite the paper's phrasing.\\\\n   112\\\\t\\\\n   113\\\\tFile-split PRs are a common place where this rule matters: new leaves should be\\\\n   114\\\\tnamed by the mathematical boundary they isolate, and any public declarations\\\\n   115\\\\texposed by the split should be reviewed under the same standard.\\\\n   116\\\\t\\\\n   117\\\\t### Shared helpers\\\\n   118\\\\t\\\\n   119\\\\tWhen the same (or nearly the same) helper declaration appears in two or\\\\n   120\\\\tmore chapter-local leaves, the copies should be consolidated into a single\\\\n   121\\\\tshared helper in an appropriate common module.  This avoids duplication and\\\\n   122\\\\tensures that fixes, generalizations, or upstream replacements apply to all\\\\n   123\\\\tcall sites at once.  Issue [#1145] tracks this work across the repository.\\\\n   124\\\\t\\\\n   125\\\\tProcedure:\\\\n   126\\\\t\\\\n   127\\\\t1. **Confirm equivalence.**  Compare the statement of each copy.  If the\\\\n   128\\\\t   types differ only by a chapter-specific parameterization (e.g.,\\\\n   129\\\\t   `SubMeas (Polynomial params) ι` vs `SubMeas (Point params) ι`),\\\\n   130\\\\t   parameterize the shared helper over that type via a `variable` or\\\\n   131\\\\t   explicit argument.\\\\n   132\\\\t2. **Find the right home.**  Prefer a module that is already imported by\\\\n   133\\\\t   both call sites.  Typical candidates:\\\\n   134\\\\t   - `MIPStarRE/LDT/Basic/` for parameter-free operators and bounds,\\\\n   135\\\\t   - `MIPStarRE/LDT/Preliminaries/` for polynomial/field/character lemmas,\\\\n   136\\\\t   - The highest chapter that is a common dependency of the two sites.\\\\n   137\\\\t3. **Name mathematically.**  Give the shared helper a name that describes\\\\n   138\\\\t   what it states, not which chapter it originally came from.\\\\n   139\\\\t4. **Replace call sites.**  Delete the chapter-local copies and replace\\\\n   140\\\\t   their uses with the shared helper.  A thin wrapper is allowed only when it\\\\n   141\\\\t   preserves a paper-facing or blueprint-facing name that external readers\\\\n   142\\\\t   need to search for; such wrappers should have a docstring or compatibility\\\\n   143\\\\t   comment explaining why the alias exists.  Remove the wrapper once that\\\\n   144\\\\t   paper-facing name is no longer needed for external reference.\\\\n   145\\\\t5. **Document.**  Add a docstring to the shared helper citing the paper or\\\\n   146\\\\t   blueprint location it supports.\\\\n   147\\\\t\\\\n   148\\\\t### Legacy identifier treatment\\\\n   149\\\\t\\\\n   150\\\\tIf a docstring must mention a legacy Lean identifier, cite it in backticks and\\\\n   151\\\\tdescribe the mathematical object in paper terminology. If an old public\\\\n   152\\\\tidentifier cannot be renamed in the current PR, record the required migration\\\\n   153\\\\tin the issue, PR description, or an audit file under `audits/`.\\\\n   154\\\\t\\\\n   155\\\\tDo not add an empty pass-through abbreviation merely to introduce a second\\\\n   156\\\\tpublic name.  This is different from a documented public wrapper allowed in the\\\\n   157\\\\tshared-helper procedure above: a wrapper must preserve a paper-facing or\\\\n   158\\\\tblueprint-facing term and must say why the alias exists.\\\\n   159\\\\t\\\\n   160\\\\t## Review Checklist\\\\n   161\\\\t\\\\n   162\\\\tReviewers should use this checklist when examining public names and\\\\n   163\\\\tdocumentation prose.  Flag any item that fails.\\\\n   164\\\\t\\\\n   165\\\\t- [ ] **Naming.**  Do public declaration names, namespace names, module names,\\\\n   166\\\\t      theorem field names, and docstring prose describe a mathematical object,\\\\n   167\\\\t      hypothesis, or conclusion?  Or do they encode the history of how the\\\\n   168\\\\t      formalization was assembled (paper line numbers, workflow steps,\\\\n   169\\\\t      implementation-phase labels)?\\\\n   170\\\\t- [ ] **Source alignment.**  For the mathematical content under review, does\\\\n   171\\\\t      the prose use the terminology in `references/ldt-paper/` or the\\\\n   172\\\\t      blueprint?  Are process words such as `pipeline`, `wrapper`, `package`,\\\\n   173\\\\t      `raw`, `oneShot`, or `liveBlock` used as mathematical terms when the\\\\n   174\\\\t      source does not use them that way?\\\\n   175\\\\t- [ ] **Deviation documented.**  If a Lean name or representation differs from\\\\n   176\\\\t      the paper, is the deviation documented in a declaration docstring,\\\\n   177\\\\t      module docstring, blueprint paragraph, or `docs/paper-gaps/` note, in\\\\n   178\\\\t      that order of preference?\\\\n   179\\\\t- [ ] **Auxiliary lemmas.**  Are formalization-only auxiliary lemmas introduced\\\\n   180\\\\t      as such, with prose naming the nearby paper equation, theorem, or\\\\n   181\\\\t      construction they support?\\\\n   182\\\\t- [ ] **Shared helpers.**  Does the PR introduce a helper that already exists\\\\n   183\\\\t      (or near-exists) in another chapter-local leaf?  If so, has it been\\\\n   184\\\\t      consolidated into a common module with a mathematical name?\\\\n   185\\\\t- [ ] **Legacy IDs.**  If a legacy Lean identifier is mentioned, is the\\\\n   186\\\\t      mathematical object described in paper terminology?  If an old public\\\\n   187\\\\t      identifier cannot be renamed yet, is the required migration recorded in\\\\n   188\\\\t      the issue, PR description, or an audit file?\\\\n   189\\\\t- [ ] **Pass-through names.**  Does the PR add an empty pass-through\\\\n   190\\\\t      abbreviation that introduces a second public name without adding\\\\n   191\\\\t      mathematical content?  Reject it unless it is a documented compatibility\\\\n   192\\\\t      wrapper preserving paper-facing or blueprint-facing terminology.\\\\n   193\\\\t\\\\n   194\\\\tReview-fix PRs touching a surface covered by an active audit should read the\\\\n   195\\\\trelevant audit before changing names or prose.\\\\n   196\\\\t\\\\n   197\\\\t[#1145]: https://github.com/LionSR/MIPStarRE/issues/1145\\\\n     1\\\\t# Blueprint Style Guide & Lessons Learned\\\\n     2\\\\t\\\\n     3\\\\t## Core Philosophy\\\\n     4\\\\tThe blueprint is a **bridge between the mathematics and the Lean formalization**. A reader should be able to read a blueprint entry and immediately understand the corresponding Lean declaration. Conversely, someone reading the Lean code should find the blueprint proof sketch faithful to what the code actually does.\\\\n     5\\\\t\\\\n     6\\\\t## General Principles\\\\n     7\\\\t1. **Blueprint ↔ Lean must match.** Every `\\\\\\\\lean{X}` tag must correspond to an actual Lean declaration. Every proof sketch must match what the Lean proof actually does — not a hand-wavy version of it.\\\\n     8\\\\t2. **Standalone.** `blueprint/` and any slides are independent — no cross-references, no shared files. Each has its own macros, its own `references.bib`.\\\\n     9\\\\t3. **Mathematical language only — zero Lean jargon.** No Lean identifiers in prose (no `selfImprovement_step`, no `PastingLemma.aux`, no `rintro ⟨X, hX⟩`). The `\\\\\\\\lean{...}` tag is the link; the body text is standard mathematics. If you can't say it in math, rewrite it.\\\\n    10\\\\t4. **No filler prose.** Only precise definitions, theorem statements, and proof sketches. No \\\\\\\"this is important because...\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"the self-improvement step governs the induction...\\\\\\\".\\\\n    11\\\\t5. **Cite non-trivial things.** Basic definitions don't need citations. Important results and non-obvious definitions should cite the source paper.\\\\n    12\\\\t6. **Don't invent terminology or notation.** Don't create ad-hoc notation when standard notation exists. Don't name things that the literature doesn't name.\\\\n    13\\\\t7. **Match Lean's theorem/lemma/def exactly.** If Lean says `theorem X`, use `\\\\\\\\begin{theorem}`. If Lean says `lemma X`, use `\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}`. `\\\\\\\\begin{proposition}` is allowed in blueprint prose when it improves mathematical readability, but still map it to an actual Lean `theorem`/`lemma` declaration via `\\\\\\\\lean{...}`. Label prefix: `thm:` for theorem, `lem:` for lemma, `def:` for definition, `prop:` for proposition.\\\\n    14\\\\t\\\\n    15\\\\t### Remark On Mathematical Prose\\\\n    16\\\\t\\\\n    17\\\\tBlueprint entries should read as mathematical exposition, not as progress\\\\n    18\\\\treports. The mathematical argument should determine the order of the prose:\\\\n    19\\\\tintroduce the notation, state the assertion, give the proof or calculation, and\\\\n    20\\\\tthen record Lean references through the usual blueprint tags. Lean declarations,\\\\n    21\\\\tfile paths, and proof-status information should not become the visible structure\\\\n    22\\\\tof the paragraph. When referring to a source article, cite it bibliographically\\\\n    23\\\\trather than relying on an uncited reference to the article.\\\\n    24\\\\t\\\\n    25\\\\tUse displayed formulae when the formula benefits from visual separation, and\\\\n    26\\\\tkeep short scalar definitions or elementary inequalities inline when the\\\\n    27\\\\tsentence reads better that way. As in ordinary mathematical writing, displayed\\\\n    28\\\\tformulae should be punctuated as part of the sentences in which they occur.\\\\n    29\\\\t\\\\n    30\\\\t## Proof Sketches Must Match Lean\\\\n    31\\\\tThis is the most important rule. Every proof in the blueprint must faithfully describe what the Lean proof does:\\\\n    32\\\\t\\\\n    33\\\\t- **Reference the actual lemmas used.** If the Lean proof calls `LDT.selfImprovement`, the blueprint proof should say \\\\\\\"By Lemma X.Y (self-improvement)...\\\\\\\" and list it in `\\\\\\\\uses`.\\\\n    34\\\\t- **Do not use `\\\\\\\\cref` or `\\\\\\\\Cref`.** The blueprint does not load `cleveref`.\\\\n    35\\\\t  Spell out the kind of object and use ordinary references, for example\\\\n    36\\\\t  `Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:self-improvement}` or `Theorem~\\\\\\\\ref{thm:main-formal}`.\\\\n    37\\\\t- **Describe the actual proof structure.** If Lean does induction on a parameter, say so. If Lean uses a specific decomposition, name it.\\\\n    38\\\\t- **Don't hand-wave where Lean is specific.** \\\\\\\"Standard argument\\\\\\\" is not acceptable if Lean uses three specific lemmas. Name them.\\\\n    39\\\\t- **Don't be more specific than Lean.** If Lean uses `simp` to close a goal, a one-line sketch is fine.\\\\n    40\\\\t- **`\\\\\\\\uses` in proofs must be accurate.** Only list what the proof actually uses, not what the statement mentions.\\\\n    41\\\\t\\\\n    42\\\\t## Source-Labelled Statements And Conditional Helpers\\\\n    43\\\\t\\\\n    44\\\\tIf a blueprint theorem, lemma, or proposition carries a source label from the\\\\n    45\\\\tpaper, the linked Lean declaration must state the same theorem, up to faithful\\\\n    46\\\\tformal encoding of the paper's domain.  Do not use `\\\\\\\\lean{...}` and `\\\\\\\\leanok`\\\\n    47\\\\ton the source-labelled statement to point to a conditional helper whose\\\\n    48\\\\tadditional assumptions supply an unproved part of the paper proof.\\\\n    49\\\\tChanging the Lean statement away from `references/ldt-paper/` is strongly\\\\n    50\\\\tdiscouraged unless faithful formal encoding or a documented mathematical\\\\n    51\\\\tnecessity requires it; the blueprint should make any such necessity explicit.\\\\n    52\\\\t\\\\n    53\\\\tBoundary hypotheses such as nonemptiness, decidability, field-model instances,\\\\n    54\\\\tpositivity of parameters, and denominator nonvanishing may be faithful when\\\\n    55\\\\tthey express the mathematical domain that the paper leaves implicit.  By\\\\n    56\\\\tcontrast, bridge inputs, residual data, repair hypotheses, producer\\\\n    57\\\\tassumptions, proof-obligation inputs, or package assumptions are proof debt.\\\\n    58\\\\tThey must not be added to a source-labelled theorem statement.  If such data is\\\\n    59\\\\tstill unproved, the source-labelled Lean theorem should keep the source-shaped\\\\n    60\\\\tstatement and carry a tracked `sorry`, or the missing assertion should appear\\\\n    61\\\\tas the lowest named internal obligation with a tracked `sorry`.  A separately\\\\n    62\\\\tnamed conditional helper should not be introduced as a repair pattern.  If one\\\\n    63\\\\talready contains substantial proof content, it may remain temporarily only as\\\\n    64\\\\tquarantine: it needs a paper source, a tracking issue, and a discharge or\\\\n    65\\\\tdeletion plan.  The blueprint should leave the paper theorem unmarked as\\\\n    66\\\\tproof-complete until the source-faithful Lean statement has a source-faithful\\\\n    67\\\\tproof.\\\\n    68\\\\t\\\\n    69\\\\tIf a Lean theorem proves only a restricted version of a source theorem, the\\\\n    70\\\\tblueprint entry carrying `\\\\\\\\leanok` must state that restriction in the theorem\\\\n    71\\\\tstatement itself.  It must not be cited as the unrestricted source theorem.\\\\n    72\\\\tWhen the only Lean declaration available for a source label has extra\\\\n    73\\\\tnon-paper assumptions, remove the `\\\\\\\\leanok` claim from the source-labelled\\\\n    74\\\\tentry or move the restricted statement to a separate Lean-only entry.  The\\\\n    75\\\\tunrestricted paper theorem remains unformalized until a Lean statement with\\\\n    76\\\\tthe source's hypothesis set exists.\\\\n    77\\\\t\\\\n    78\\\\t## Notation Consistency\\\\n    79\\\\tNotation must be **internally consistent** across the entire blueprint and **close to what the Lean code expresses**:\\\\n    80\\\\t\\\\n    81\\\\t- **Indices**: 0-indexed where matching `Fin n` in Lean\\\\n    82\\\\t- **Finite fields / alphabets**: use standard notation for the alphabet $\\\\\\\\Sigma$ and finite fields $\\\\\\\\mathbb{F}_q$\\\\n    83\\\\t- **Measurements**: use standard quantum measurement notation (projective measurements, POVMs)\\\\n    84\\\\t- **Strategies**: quantum strategies for nonlocal games should use standard notation from the literature\\\\n    85\\\\t- **Expansion**: use standard notation for graph expansion parameters\\\\n    86\\\\t- **Macros** (in `macros/common.tex`): define project-specific macros and use them consistently\\\\n    87\\\\t\\\\n    88\\\\tThe original reference controls notation whenever it gives a usable convention.\\\\n    89\\\\tIf the blueprint needs a different symbol, index convention, or name in order to\\\\n    90\\\\tmatch Lean, introduce it explicitly and relate it to the paper notation in the\\\\n    91\\\\tsame paragraph.  Do not let a `\\\\\\\\lean{...}` tag, a Lean namespace, or a file name\\\\n    92\\\\tbe the only explanation of a changed term.\\\\n    93\\\\t\\\\n    94\\\\tWhen a public Lean declaration is a formalization-only auxiliary lemma, the\\\\n    95\\\\tblueprint should say so before listing the declaration.  The surrounding prose\\\\n    96\\\\tshould identify the paper theorem, equation, or construction that the auxiliary\\\\n    97\\\\tlemma supports, and should avoid making the auxiliary name look like a named\\\\n    98\\\\tresult from the source article.\\\\n    99\\\\t\\\\n   100\\\\t## What NOT to Put in the Blueprint\\\\n   101\\\\t- **Lean identifier names in math text.** Write \\\\\\\"the self-improvement lemma\\\\\\\", never \\\\\\\"the `selfImprovement` step\\\\\\\". The `\\\\\\\\lean{LDT.selfImprovement}` tag handles the linking.\\\\n   102\\\\t- **Implementation details.** Don't say \\\\\\\"bundled as an element of the Euclidean space\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"using `EuclideanSpace.equiv`\\\\\\\". Describe the mathematical object.\\\\n   103\\\\t- **Ad-hoc notation.** Don't invent superscripts or subscripts to distinguish from existing notation. Use standard conventions.\\\\n   104\\\\t- **Function-call syntax.** Use mathematical notation, not programming notation.\\\\n   105\\\\t- **Redundant definitions.** If two blueprint definitions describe the same mathematical object, consolidate them. Each definition should introduce genuinely new mathematical content.\\\\n   106\\\\t- **Lean namespace prefixes in prose.** Don't write \\\\\\\"the `LDT.PastingLemma`\\\\\\\" — write \\\\\\\"the pasting lemma\\\\\\\".\\\\n   107\\\\t\\\\n   108\\\\t## Banned AI/Software Language (enforced in both blueprint AND Lean code)\\\\n   109\\\\tThe blueprint reads as a **mathematical document**, not software documentation. The following patterns are banned in ALL reader-facing text (section titles, theorem names, proof sketches, remarks, chapter preambles) and in ALL Lean docstrings, comments, and section names:\\\\n   110\\\\t\\\\n   111\\\\t### Banned terms → replacements:\\\\n   112\\\\t| Banned | Use instead |\\\\n   113\\\\t|--------|------------|\\\\n   114\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Assembly\\\\\\\" (as section/chapter title) | \\\\\\\"Proof of [theorem]\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Construction\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Composition\\\\\\\" |\\\\n   115\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Pipeline\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"reduction\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"construction\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"proof chain\\\\\\\" |\\\\n   116\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Bridge\\\\\\\" (as noun for a connection) | \\\\\\\"connection\\\\\\\", name by mathematical content |\\\\n   117\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Handoff\\\\\\\" / \\\\\\\"hand off\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"transition\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"continuation\\\\\\\", or drop entirely |\\\\n   118\\\\t| \\\\\\\"In this blueprint\\\\\\\" / \\\\\\\"Within this blueprint\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"here\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"in what follows\\\\\\\", or omit |\\\\n   119\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Honest\\\\\\\" (as adjective for rigorous) | \\\\\\\"exact\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"faithful\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"unconditional\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"complete\\\\\\\" |\\\\n   120\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Glue layer\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"intermediate construction\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"connecting results\\\\\\\" |\\\\n   121\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Re-export\\\\\\\" / \\\\\\\"reexport\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"provides\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"re-states\\\\\\\" |\\\\n   122\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Wiring\\\\\\\" / \\\\\\\"wire up\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"connecting\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"composing\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"combining\\\\\\\" |\\\\n   123\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Package\\\\\\\" (as noun for a data bundle) | \\\\\\\"form\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"structure\\\\\\\" |\\\\n   124\\\\t| \\\\\\\"Sorry-free\\\\\\\" | acceptable only in Lean-specific technical context |\\\\n   125\\\\t\\\\n   126\\\\t### Additional rules:\\\\n   127\\\\t- **Section names in Lean**: use mathematical terms (`section QuantumSoundness`, `section SelfImprovement`, `section PastingLemma`), not organizational terms (`section Assembly`, `section Pipeline`).\\\\n   128\\\\t- **Internal LaTeX labels** (e.g., `\\\\\\\\label{ch:soundness}`) are not reader-facing and need not be renamed if doing so would break cross-references. But NEW labels should follow the standard.\\\\n   129\\\\t- **Definitions that are actually theorems**: if a statement asserts a mathematical fact, it must be `\\\\\\\\begin{theorem}`, not `\\\\\\\\begin{definition}`. Definitions introduce new objects; theorems prove properties.\\\\n   130\\\\t\\\\n   131\\\\t## `\\\\\\\\uses` Dependency Guidelines\\\\n   132\\\\t- **Statement `\\\\\\\\uses`**: list only what's needed to *state* the result (typically definitions of the objects involved). Keep minimal — transitive deps are automatic.\\\\n   133\\\\t- **Proof `\\\\\\\\uses`**: list only what the proof *actually calls*. Don't list definitions unless the proof unfolds that definition.\\\\n   134\\\\t- **Never self-reference**: a proof's `\\\\\\\\uses` must NOT include the label of the theorem it proves.\\\\n   135\\\\t\\\\n   136\\\\t## Recording Formalization-Only Lemmas\\\\n   137\\\\tSome Lean lemmas are genuine proof infrastructure rather than named statements\\\\n   138\\\\tfrom the original papers. These must be traceable without making the blueprint\\\\n   139\\\\tlook as if the paper stated them.\\\\n   140\\\\t\\\\n   141\\\\t- If a declaration directly formalizes a paper or blueprint statement, record\\\\n   142\\\\t  it as usual with `\\\\\\\\lean{...}` on that statement and cite the relevant source.\\\\n   143\\\\t- If a public declaration is an auxiliary lemma introduced only for the Lean\\\\n   144\\\\t  proof, make that status explicit. Its Lean docstring should say that it is a\\\\n   145\\\\t  formalization-only auxiliary lemma and should name the nearby paper equation,\\\\n   146\\\\t  line range, or blueprint result it supports.\\\\n   147\\\\t- If such an auxiliary declaration is important enough to be referenced from\\\\n   148\\\\t  the blueprint, give it a clearly subordinate blueprint node, for example\\\\n   149\\\\t  `[Formalization support for ...]`, and state in the first sentence that the\\\\n   150\\\\t  lemma is not a named statement of the paper but isolates a step used in the\\\\n   151\\\\t  Lean proof.\\\\n   152\\\\t- Connect auxiliary nodes to the corresponding result from the paper with `\\\\\\\\uses{...}` from\\\\n   153\\\\t  the proof that actually calls them. Do not add them only to silence a sync\\\\n   154\\\\t  warning; the dependency edge should explain where the auxiliary result is\\\\n   155\\\\t  used.\\\\n   156\\\\t- Do not mark an auxiliary node with proof-level `\\\\\\\\leanok` unless its Lean proof\\\\n   157\\\\t  is complete and `#print axioms` has no `sorryAx`. Statement-level `\\\\\\\\leanok`\\\\n   158\\\\t  is acceptable only when the Lean statement is intentionally recorded but the\\\\n   159\\\\t  proof remains tracked as incomplete.\\\\n   160\\\\t\\\\n   161\\\\t## Blueprint Structure\\\\n   162\\\\t- `content.tex` is a router: `\\\\\\\\input{chapter/ch01_intro}` etc.\\\\n   163\\\\t- Each chapter is a separate file in `chapter/`\\\\n   164\\\\t- Definitions/theorems numbered within chapters: `\\\\\\\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[chapter]`\\\\n   165\\\\t\\\\n   166\\\\t## Lean Blueprint Macros\\\\n   167\\\\t- `\\\\\\\\lean{Namespace.DeclName}` — links to Lean declaration\\\\n   168\\\\t- `\\\\\\\\leanok` — context-sensitive marker; see \\\\\\\"Statement-level vs proof-level `\\\\\\\\leanok`\\\\\\\" below\\\\n   169\\\\t- `\\\\\\\\uses{label1, label2}` — declares dependency edges for the graph\\\\n   170\\\\t- `\\\\\\\\notready` — marks as not ready for formalization (orange in graph)\\\\n   171\\\\t- `\\\\\\\\mathlibok` — already in Mathlib (dark green in graph; see legend note below)\\\\n   172\\\\t\\\\n   173\\\\t### Statement-level vs proof-level `\\\\\\\\leanok`\\\\n   174\\\\t\\\\n   175\\\\t`\\\\\\\\leanok` is context-sensitive for human review and graph interpretation. The same macro is used for two different claims, so reviewers should read it by placement rather than by raw token count.\\\\n   176\\\\t\\\\n   177\\\\t**Statement-level `\\\\\\\\leanok`.** Place this inside a `theorem`, `lemma`, `proposition`, `corollary`, or `definition` environment, on its own line immediately after `\\\\\\\\lean{...}`. It says that the corresponding Lean declaration exists and that its statement matches the blueprint or paper statement. It does **not** say that the Lean proof already exists or is complete. For definitions, this is the only `\\\\\\\\leanok` marker.\\\\n   178\\\\t\\\\n   179\\\\t**Proof-level `\\\\\\\\leanok`.** Place this inside the matching `proof` environment, preferably as the first line after `\\\\\\\\begin{proof}`. The canonical current placement is the theorem `thm:commutativity-points` in `blueprint/src/chapter/ch08_commutativity.tex:8-19`, where the statement block has `\\\\\\\\leanok` immediately after `\\\\\\\\lean{...}` and the proof block begins with another `\\\\\\\\leanok`. This marker says that the Lean proof is complete and free of unresolved `sorry` or unjustified project-level `axiom` shortcuts. In particular, `#print axioms <decl>` must show no `sorryAx`, and the declaration must satisfy the broader blocker rules in the lean-conventions `PROOF_INTEGRITY` reference and [`docs/project_conventions.md`](project_conventions.md).\\\\n   180\\\\t\\\\n   181\\\\tA theorem, lemma, proposition, or corollary node is **fully formalized in Lean if and only if it has both markers**: statement-level `\\\\\\\\leanok` in the statement block and proof-level `\\\\\\\\leanok` in the proof block. Statement-level without proof-level means “the statement has been transcribed and matched, but the proof is not yet certified complete.” Definitions have no proof block, so statement-level `\\\\\\\\leanok` alone is enough there.\\\\n   182\\\\t\\\\n   183\\\\tExcerpt from `blueprint/src/chapter/ch08_commutativity.tex:8-19`:\\\\n   184\\\\t\\\\n   185\\\\t```latex\\\\n   186\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{theorem}[Commutativity of the point measurements]\\\\\\\\label{thm:commutativity-points}\\\\n   187\\\\t  \\\\\\\\lean{MIPStarRE.LDT.CommutativityPoints.commutativityPoints}\\\\n   188\\\\t  \\\\\\\\leanok\\\\n   189\\\\t  \\\\\\\\uses{def:good-strategy, def:approx_delta}\\\\n   190\\\\t  Let $(\\\\\\\\psi,A,B,L)$ be an $(\\\\\\\\eps,\\\\\\\\delta,\\\\\\\\gamma)$-good symmetric strategy.\\\\n   191\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{theorem}\\\\n   192\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\n   193\\\\t  \\\\\\\\leanok\\\\n   194\\\\t  The strategy passes the diagonal lines test with probability $1-\\\\\\\\gamma$.\\\\n   195\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n   196\\\\t```\\\\n   197\\\\t\\\\n   198\\\\t**Current CI behavior.** The advisory Blueprint ↔ Lean sync check in [`docs/ci-blueprint-sync.md`](ci-blueprint-sync.md) distinguishes the two placements. Only proof-level `\\\\\\\\leanok` whose axiom closure contains `sorryAx` (or whose harness output cannot be parsed) is reported as an **error**. Statement-level-only `\\\\\\\\leanok` with the same finding is downgraded to a **warning**, because statement-level does not claim proof completeness. Each audit line is annotated with the observed placement so reviewers can tell statement-sync work apart from proof-completion work.\\\\n   199\\\\t\\\\n   200\\\\t`leanblueprint` does not use distinct macro names for these two claims, so audits must distinguish them by environment context rather than by raw `\\\\\\\\leanok` counts. A future split such as `\\\\\\\\leanokstmt` / `\\\\\\\\leanokproof` could make that distinction explicit, but that is only a possible later cleanup; the current convention is to keep `\\\\\\\\leanok` and rely on placement.\\\\n   201\\\\t\\\\n   202\\\\tA recurring historical example is the chapter 4 `Q/X/\\\\\\\\widehat X/P` witness\\\\n   203\\\\tlayer.  Once Lean has construction theorems deriving the displayed identities\\\\n   204\\\\tand estimates from the rank-reduction, sigma-range, positive-Gram, native\\\\n   205\\\\tspectral-theorem, or rectangular-SVD hypotheses, the corresponding blueprint\\\\n   206\\\\tentries may carry `\\\\\\\\leanok`.  A declaration that merely projects an identity\\\\n   207\\\\tfrom an opaque data record is not enough by itself, and a theorem depending on\\\\n   208\\\\textra rectangular-SVD data should not be used as evidence for an unconditional\\\\n   209\\\\tpaper statement unless a separate construction supplies that data.\\\\n   210\\\\t\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr184-20260905-04.jsonl-339-{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_173\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The remaining chapter-16 declarations are intentionally open obligations, so their absent completion marks are accurate. One status issue remains elsewhere: the Chapter 15 combined-points lemma is still tagged `\\\\\\\\notready` even though its Lean declaration has the same source-shaped signature; I’m now doing a final changed-line scan for terminology and dependency-tag issues.\\\"}}\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr184-20260905-04.jsonl:340:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\