Re \\\\\\\\Tr\\\\\\\\big(\\\\\\\\mathcal{O}^a \\\\\\\\, (\\\\\\\\sum_u \\\\\\\\chi_u(a) B^u) \\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\rho\\\\\\\\big)$.\\\\n   795\\\\t\\\\tAveraging over $a$ and applying the overlap and cubic identities of\\\\n   796\\\\t\\\\tLemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:fourier-square-measurement} turns the average into\\\\n   797\\\\t\\\\t$2 - 2 \\\\\\\\, C_\\\\\\\\rho(\\\\\\\\mathcal{O})$, which is the mean multiplicative defect by\\\\n   798\\\\t\\\\tLemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:blr-defect-identity}. No commutation among the\\\\n   799\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\mathcal{O}^u$ is used. The hypothesis then bounds the average by $\\\\\\\\eta$.\\\\n   800\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n   801\\\\t\\\\n   802\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Combined point measurements]\\\\n   803\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{lem:qld-4-10}\\\\n   804\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.CombinedPointsWitness}\\\\n   805\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_combinedPointsWitness}\\\\n   806\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\notready\\\\n   807\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:expanded-state, def:expanded-point-measurement,\\\\n   808\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tdef:povm-distance, def:symmetric-equivalents}\\\\n   809\\\\t\\\\tThere exists a function $\\\\\\\\delta_Q(\\\\\\\\eps) = \\\\\\\\poly(\\\\\\\\eps)$ such that the\\\\n   810\\\\t\\\\tfollowing holds. For every $x, z \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m$ and\\\\n   811\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\hilb \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hilb_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}}, \\\\\\\\hilb_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{B}}\\\\\\\\}$ there are\\\\n   812\\\\t\\\\tprojective measurements $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\\\\\\\\}_{a,b \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q}$ acting\\\\n   813\\\\t\\\\ton $\\\\\\\\hilb \\\\\\\\ot (\\\\\\\\C^q)^{\\\\\\\\ot M}$ such that:\\\\n   814\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\n   815\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\item (\\\\\\\\textbf{Self-consistency}) On average over uniformly random\\\\n   816\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t$x, z \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m$,\\\\n   817\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\n   818\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{eq:qld-q-self-cons}\\\\n   819\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}'}\\\\n   820\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\delta_Q}\\\\n   821\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{B}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''}\\\\\\\\,.\\\\n   822\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\n   823\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\item (\\\\\\\\textbf{Consistency with $\\\\\\\\hat{M}$}) On average over uniformly\\\\n   824\\\\t\\\\t\\\\trandom $x, z \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m$,\\\\n   825\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{gather}\\\\n   826\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-xz}\\\\n   827\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}'}\\\\n   828\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\delta_Q}\\\\n   829\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a} \\\\\\\\,\\\\n   830\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b})_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{B}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''}\\\\\\\\,,\\\\n   831\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   832\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx}\\\\n   833\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}'}\\\\n   834\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\delta_Q}\\\\n   835\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\\\\\\\,\\\\n   836\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a})_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{B}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''}\\\\\\\\,.\\\\n   837\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{gather}\\\\n   838\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\n   839\\\\t\\\\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also hold.\\\\n   840\\\\t\\\\tThe operators $\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}$ and\\\\n   841\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b}$ do not commute in general, so the\\\\n   842\\\\t\\\\tproducts in \\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-xz} and\\\\n   843\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx} differ and the two relations are separate\\\\n   844\\\\t\\\\tconclusions.\\\\n   845\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\n   846\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\n   847\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{lem:qld-comm-cons, def:expanded-point-trace-projection,\\\\n   848\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tfact:add-a-proj, fact:agreement, lem:ortho, thm:linearity,\\\\n   849\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tlem:avg-closeness, lem:cancellation, def:povm-distance}\\\\n   850\\\\t\\\\tThroughout the proof every intermediate error is a $\\\\\\\\poly(\\\\\\\\eps)$ function\\\\n   851\\\\t\\\\tthat may differ from one use of $\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\delta$ to the next. For\\\\n   852\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\omega = (x,z,r,s) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\F_q$\\\\n   853\\\\t\\\\tand $a, b \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_2$ define the sandwich POVM\\\\n   854\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\hat{R}^{\\\\\\\\omega}_{a,b}\\\\n   855\\\\t\\\\t= \\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},Z),z,s}_{b}\\\\n   856\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},X),x,r}_{a}\\\\n   857\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},Z),z,s}_{b}$\\\\n   858\\\\t\\\\tfrom the binary refinements of\\\\n   859\\\\t\\\\tDefinition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:expanded-point-trace-projection}; by the approximate\\\\n   860\\\\t\\\\tcommutation of Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}, projectivity, and\\\\n     1\\\\timport MIPStarRE.QPBT.Combining.Witnesses\\\\n     2\\\\t\\\\n     3\\\\t/-!\\\\n     4\\\\t# Combining the point measurements\\\\n     5\\\\t\\\\n     6\\\\tThis module states the construction obligation for the joint X/Z point\\\\n     7\\\\tmeasurements and defines their scalar linear coarse-graining.  The latter is\\\\n     8\\\\tthe genuine postprocessing of a complete measurement on each heterogeneous\\\\n     9\\\\tplayer space.\\\\n    10\\\\t\\\\n    11\\\\t## References\\\\n    12\\\\t\\\\n    13\\\\tThe construction is `lem:qld-4-10` in\\\\n    14\\\\t`blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex`, with paper source\\\\n    15\\\\t`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:689-709`.\\\\n    16\\\\tThe coarse-graining is `lem:qld-4-12` in the same blueprint, with paper source\\\\n    17\\\\t`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:993-1011`.\\\\n    18\\\\t-/\\\\n    19\\\\t\\\\n    20\\\\topen scoped BigOperators\\\\n    21\\\\t\\\\n    22\\\\tnamespace MIPStarRE.QPBT\\\\n    23\\\\t\\\\n    24\\\\topen MIPStarRE.LDT hiding Measurement\\\\n    25\\\\topen MIPStarRE.Quantum\\\\n    26\\\\t\\\\n    27\\\\tnoncomputable section\\\\n    28\\\\t\\\\n    29\\\\t/-- A pair of source points and the two scalar coefficients used by the\\\\n    30\\\\textended point measurement. -/\\\\n    31\\\\tabbrev ExtendedPointQuestion (P : AdmissibleParams) :=\\\\n    32\\\\t  ((Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P) × (Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P)) ×\\\\n    33\\\\t    (PauliScalar P × PauliScalar P)\\\\n    34\\\\t\\\\n    35\\\\tnamespace CombinedPointsWitness\\\\n    36\\\\t\\\\n    37\\\\t/-- Coarse-grain a joint point measurement by `(a,b) |-> alpha*a + beta*b`\\\\n    38\\\\ton the selected player side.  This is the concrete measurement of\\\\n    39\\\\t`lem:qld-4-12`, paper lines 993--1011. -/\\\\n    40\\\\tnoncomputable def extendedQ {P : AdmissibleParams} {ε δ : ℝ}\\\\n    41\\\\t    {S : ProjectiveSetting P ε} (points : CombinedPointsWitness S δ)\\\\n    42\\\\t    (side : PlayerSide) (x z : Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P)\\\\n    43\\\\t    (alpha beta : PauliScalar P) :\\\\n    44\\\\t    Measurement (PauliScalar P) (S.ExpandedLocalSpace side) :=\\\\n    45\\\\t  (points.Q side x z).postprocess fun ab => alpha * ab.1 + beta * ab.2\\\\n    46\\\\t\\\\n    47\\\\tend CombinedPointsWitness\\\\n    48\\\\t\\\\n    49\\\\t/-- Construction of the projective joint point measurements of\\\\n    50\\\\t`lem:qld-4-10`, paper lines 689--709.  The witness retains both ordered\\\\n    51\\\\tproducts and all four directed heterogeneous placement comparisons. -/\\\\n    52\\\\ttheorem exists_combinedPointsWitness :\\\\n    53\\\\t    ∃ deltaQ : ℝ -> ℝ, IsPolyErr deltaQ ∧\\\\n    54\\\\t      ∀ (P : AdmissibleParams) (ε : ℝ) (S : ProjectiveSetting P ε),\\\\n    55\\\\t        Nonempty (CombinedPointsWitness S (deltaQ ε)) := by\\\\n    56\\\\t  sorry\\\\n    57\\\\t\\\\n    58\\\\t/-- Projectivity and the three data-processed consistency guarantees for\\\\n    59\\\\t`CombinedPointsWitness.extendedQ`.  This is `lem:qld-4-12`, paper lines\\\\n    60\\\\t993--1011; the `XZ` and `ZX` source products remain separate. -/\\\\n    61\\\\ttheorem extendedQ_spec {P : AdmissibleParams} {ε δ : ℝ}\\\\n    62\\\\t    {S : ProjectiveSetting P ε} (points : CombinedPointsWitness S δ) :\\\\n    63\\\\t    (∀ side x z alpha beta,\\\\n    64\\\\t      Measurement.IsProjective (points.extendedQ side x z alpha beta)) ∧\\\\n    65\\\\t    (∀ p1 p2 : Placement, p1.IsOpposite p2 ->\\\\n    66\\\\t      opFamilyDistSq (uniformDistribution (ExtendedPointQuestion P))\\\\n    67\\\\t        (fun question c => S.place p1\\\\n    68\\\\t          ((points.extendedQ p1.side question.1.1 question.1.2\\\\n    69\\\\t            question.2.1 question.2.2).effect c))\\\\n    70\\\\t        (fun question c => S.place p2\\\\n    71\\\\t          ((points.extendedQ p2.side question.1.1 question.1.2\\\\n    72\\\\t            question.2.1 question.2.2).effect c))\\\\n    73\\\\t        S.psiHat <= δ) ∧\\\\n    74\\\\t    (∀ p1 p2 : Placement, p1.IsOpposite p2 ->\\\\n    75\\\\t      opFamilyDistSq (uniformDistribution (ExtendedPointQuestion P))\\\\n    76\\\\t        (fun question c => S.place p1\\\\n    77\\\\t          ((points.extendedQ p1.side question.1.1 question.1.2\\\\n    78\\\\t            question.2.1 question.2.2).effect c))\\\\n    79\\\\t        (fun question c => S.place p2\\\\n    80\\\\t          (∑ ab ∈ Finset.univ.filter (fun ab : PauliScalar P × PauliScalar P =>\\\\n    81\\\\t              question.2.1 * ab.1 + question.2.2 * ab.2 = c),\\\\n    82\\\\t            (S.pointMeasExp p2.side .X question.1.1).effect ab.1 *\\\\n    83\\\\t              (S.pointMeasExp p2.side .Z question.1.2).effect ab.2))\\\\n    84\\\\t        S.psiHat <= δ) ∧\\\\n    85\\\\t    ∀ p1 p2 : Placement, p1.IsOpposite p2 ->\\\\n    86\\\\t      opFamilyDistSq (uniformDistribution (ExtendedPointQuestion P))\\\\n    87\\\\t        (fun question c => S.place p1\\\\n    88\\\\t          ((points.extendedQ p1.side question.1.1 question.1.2\\\\n    89\\\\t            question.2.1 question.2.2).effect c))\\\\n    90\\\\t        (fun question c => S.place p2\\\\n    91\\\\t          (∑ ab ∈ Finset.univ.filter (fun ab : PauliScalar P × PauliScalar P =>\\\\n    92\\\\t              question.2.1 * ab.1 + question.2.2 * ab.2 = c),\\\\n    93\\\\t            (S.pointMeasExp p2.side .Z question.1.2).effect ab.2 *\\\\n    94\\\\t              (S.pointMeasExp p2.side .X question.1.1).effect ab.1))\\\\n    95\\\\t        S.psiHat <= δ := by\\\\n    96\\\\t  sorry\\\\n    97\\\\t\\\\n    98\\\\tend\\\\n    99\\\\t\\\\n   100\\\\t\\\\n   101\\\\tend MIPStarRE.QPBT\\\\n    70\\\\t      | axis base' index' baseFixed' =>\\\\n    71\\\\t          simp only [directLineDescCode, Sum.inl.injEq, Prod.mk.injEq] at h\\\\n    72\\\\t          rcases h with ⟨rfl, rfl⟩\\\\n    73\\\\t          rfl\\\\n    74\\\\t      | diagonal => simp [directLineDescCode] at h\\\\n    75\\\\t  | diagonal base index direction baseFixed prefixZero =>\\\\n    76\\\\t      cases line' with\\\\n    77\\\\t      | axis => simp [directLineDescCode] at h\\\\n    78\\\\t      | diagonal base' index' direction' baseFixed' prefixZero' =>\\\\n    79\\\\t          simp only [directLineDescCode, Sum.inr.injEq, Prod.mk.injEq] at h\\\\n    80\\\\t          rcases h with ⟨rfl, rfl, rfl⟩\\\\n    81\\\\t          rfl\\\\n    82\\\\t\\\\n    83\\\\t/-- Directly indexed line descriptions form a finite type. -/\\\\n    84\\\\tnoncomputable instance directLineDescFintype (D : DirectLdParams) :\\\\n    85\\\\t    Fintype (DirectLineDesc D) :=\\\\n    86\\\\t  Fintype.ofInjective (directLineDescCode D) (directLineDescCode_injective D)\\\\n    87\\\\t\\\\n    88\\\\t/-! ## Combined point and line measurements -/\\\\n    89\\\\t\\\\n    90\\\\t/-- Projective joint point measurements and their ordered consistency\\\\n    91\\\\tguarantees from `lem:qld-4-10`, paper lines 689--709.  The `XZ` and `ZX`\\\\n    92\\\\tproducts are retained as distinct fields. -/\\\\n    93\\\\tstructure CombinedPointsWitness {P : AdmissibleParams} {ε : ℝ}\\\\n    94\\\\t    (S : ProjectiveSetting P ε) (δ : ℝ) where\\\\n    95\\\\t  /-- The complete joint measurement on each player's expanded local space. -/\\\\n    96\\\\t  Q : (side : PlayerSide) ->\\\\n    97\\\\t    (Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P) ->\\\\n    98\\\\t    (Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P) ->\\\\n    99\\\\t    Measurement (PauliScalar P × PauliScalar P) (S.ExpandedLocalSpace side)\\\\n   100\\\\t  /-- Every joint point measurement is projective. -/\\\\n   101\\\\t  projective : ∀ side x z, Measurement.IsProjective (Q side x z)\\\\n   102\\\\t  /-- Joint point measurements agree across every directed opposite placement. -/\\\\n   103\\\\t  self_consistent : ∀ p1 p2 : Placement, p1.IsOpposite p2 ->\\\\n   104\\\\t    opFamilyDistSq\\\\n   105\\\\t      (uniformDistribution\\\\n   106\\\\t        ((Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P) × (Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P)))\\\\n   107\\\\t      (fun xz ab => S.place p1 ((Q p1.side xz.1 xz.2).effect ab))\\\\n   108\\\\t      (fun xz ab => S.place p2 ((Q p2.side xz.1 xz.2).effect ab))\\\\n   109\\\\t      S.psiHat <= δ\\\\n   110\\\\t  /-- Joint point measurements are close to the ordered `X`-then-`Z` product. -/\\\\n   111\\\\t  consistent_XZ : ∀ p1 p2 : Placement, p1.IsOpposite p2 ->\\\\n   112\\\\t    opFamilyDistSq\\\\n   113\\\\t      (uniformDistribution\\\\n   114\\\\t        ((Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P) × (Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P)))\\\\n   115\\\\t      (fun xz ab => S.place p1 ((Q p1.side xz.1 xz.2).effect ab))\\\\n   116\\\\t      (fun xz ab => S.place p2\\\\n   117\\\\t        ((S.pointMeasExp p2.side .X xz.1).effect ab.1 *\\\\n   118\\\\t          (S.pointMeasExp p2.side .Z xz.2).effect ab.2))\\\\n   119\\\\t      S.psiHat <= δ\\\\n   120\\\\t  /-- Joint point measurements are close to the ordered `Z`-then-`X` product. -/\\\\n   121\\\\t  consistent_ZX : ∀ p1 p2 : Placement, p1.IsOpposite p2 ->\\\\n   122\\\\t    opFamilyDistSq\\\\n   123\\\\t      (uniformDistribution\\\\n   124\\\\t        ((Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P) × (Fin P.m -> PauliScalar P)))\\\\n   125\\\\t      (fun xz ab => S.place p1 ((Q p1.side xz.1 xz.2).effect ab))\\\\n   126\\\\t      (fun xz ab => S.place p2\\\\n   127\\\\t        ((S.pointMeasExp p2.side .Z xz.2).effect ab.2 *\\\\n   128\\\\t          (S.pointMeasExp p2.side .X xz.1).effect ab.1))\\\\n   129\\\\t      S.psiHat <= δ\\\\n   130\\\\t\\\\n   131\\\\t/-- Joint line measurements and their evaluation consistency from\\\\n   132\\\\t`lem:qld-xz-lines`, paper lines 882--894.  Evaluation uses explicit `Option`\\\\n   133\\\\toutcomes, including the class of answers not determined at the sampled point. -/\\\\n   134\\\\tstructure CombinedLinesWitness {P : AdmissibleParams} {ε δQ : ℝ}\\\\n   135\\\\t    (S : ProjectiveSetting P ε) (points : CombinedPointsWitness S δQ)\\\\n   136\\\\t    (δ : ℝ) where\\\\n   137\\\\t  /-- The complete paired-line measurement on each expanded local space. -/\\\\n   138\\\\t  T : (side : PlayerSide) ->\\\\n   139\\\\t    LineDesc P.toLdParams -> LineDesc P.toLdParams ->\\\\n   140\\\\t    Measurement\\\\n   141\\\\t      (DegPoly P.toLdParams (P.m * P.d) ×\\\\n   142\\\\t        DegPoly P.toLdParams (P.m * P.d))\\\\n   143\\\\t      (S.ExpandedLocalSpace side)\\\\n   144\\\\t  /-- On an axis-parallel X line, outcomes outside degree `d` have zero effect. -/\\\\n   145\\\\t  axis_degree_X : ∀ side lineX lineZ fX fZ,\\\\n   146\\\\t    lineX.kind = .axis -> Not (DegPoly.FitsDegree P.d fX) ->\\\\n   147\\\\t      (T side lineX lineZ).effect (fX, fZ) = 0\\\\n   148\\\\t  /-- On an axis-parallel Z line, outcomes outside degree `d` have zero effect. -/\\\\n   149\\\\t  axis_degree_Z : ∀ side lineX lineZ fX fZ,\\\\n   150\\\\t    lineZ.kind = .axis -> Not (DegPoly.FitsDegree P.d fZ) ->\\\\n   151\\\\t      (T side lineX lineZ).effect (fX, fZ) = 0\\\\n   152\\\\t  /-- Evaluated line pairs are consistent with the completed point-pair\\\\n   153\\\\t  measurement on every directed opposite placement. -/\\\\n   154\\\\t  consistent : ∀ p1 p2 : Placement, p1.IsOpposite p2 ->\\\\n   155\\\\t    consistencyDefect\\\\n    35\\\\t\\\\n    36\\\\t/-- The two local registers of a heterogeneous bipartite strategy. This is\\\\n    37\\\\tformalization-only indexing for the player-dependent notation at paper\\\\n    38\\\\t`14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:160-190`. -/\\\\n    39\\\\tinductive PlayerSide where\\\\n    40\\\\t  | alice\\\\n    41\\\\t  | bob\\\\n    42\\\\t  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited, Fintype\\\\n    43\\\\t\\\\n    44\\\\t/-- A projective strategy satisfying the winning premise used throughout\\\\n    45\\\\tSection `sec:commutation`. The structure retains the distinct player spaces and\\\\n    46\\\\tmeasurement families of `Strategy`. Paper\\\\n    47\\\\t`14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:160-172`; blueprint\\\\n    48\\\\t`ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:385-475`. -/\\\\n    49\\\\tstructure ProjectiveSetting (P : AdmissibleParams) (ε : ℝ) where\\\\n    50\\\\t  toStrategy : Strategy (pauliBasisTest P)\\\\n    51\\\\t  isProjective : toStrategy.IsProjective\\\\n    52\\\\t  win : 1 - ε ≤ toStrategy.value\\\\n    53\\\\t\\\\n    54\\\\tnamespace ProjectiveSetting\\\\n    55\\\\t\\\\n    56\\\\tvariable {P : AdmissibleParams} {ε : ℝ}\\\\n    57\\\\t\\\\n    58\\\\t/-- The local Hilbert-space index type on a specified player side. It indexes\\\\n    59\\\\tthe side-qualified operators at paper\\\\n    60\\\\t`14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:174-190`. -/\\\\n    61\\\\tdef LocalSpace (S : ProjectiveSetting P ε) : PlayerSide → Type\\\\n    62\\\\t  | .alice => S.toStrategy.ιA\\\\n    63\\\\t  | .bob => S.toStrategy.ιB\\\\n    64\\\\t\\\\n    65\\\\tinstance localSpaceFintype (S : ProjectiveSetting P ε) (side : PlayerSide) :\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr184-20260905-04.jsonl-346-{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_177\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/bash -lc \\\\\\\"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex | sed -n '1,82p'\\\\nnl -ba MIPStarRE/QPBT/Algebra/Decoding.lean | sed -n '1,210p'\\\\nrg -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{def:decoding-map\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}|decodeAt_lowDegreeEnc|decodeAt_add|decodeAt_smul\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" blueprint/src/chapter MIPStarRE/QPBT/Algebra/Decoding.lean\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr184-20260905-04.jsonl-347-{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_177\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/bash -lc \\\\\\\"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex | sed -n '1,82p'\\\\nnl -ba MIPStarRE/QPBT/Algebra/Decoding.lean | sed -n '1,210p'\\\\nrg -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{def:decoding-map\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}|decodeAt_lowDegreeEnc|decodeAt_add|decodeAt_smul\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" blueprint/src/chapter MIPStarRE/QPBT/Algebra/Decoding.lean\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"     1\\\\t\\\\\\\\chapter{Extracting the Pauli observables}\\\\n     2\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{chap:qpbt-extraction}\\\\n     3\\\\t% Paper subsection label sec:separating kept for cross-reference fidelity.\\\\n     4\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{sec:separating}\\\\n     5\\\\t% Source: references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex,\\\\n     6\\\\t% lines 1415--1877 (subsection ``Pulling the X and Z measurements apart''\\\\n     7\\\\t% and the closing proof of the soundness theorem).\\\\n     8\\\\t\\\\n     9\\\\tThis chapter completes the analysis of the Pauli basis test~\\\\\\\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}. Throughout, $(q,m,d)$ is an admissible parameter tuple (Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:admissible}), $q = 2^t$, and $M = 2^m$ denotes the number of $q$-ary qudits tested for per player. (The letter $M$ also denotes measurement operators, always carrying super- and subscripts; the meaning is determined by context, as in the source.) We fix a projective strategy for the Pauli basis test game $\\\\\\\\mathfrak{G}^{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$ that succeeds with probability at least $1-\\\\\\\\eps$, in the setting of Chapter~\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-observables}: the shared state $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\psi}$ on registers $\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}$, $\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}$ is assumed padded with sufficiently many ancilla qubits in the state $\\\\\\\\ket{0}$ (Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:projective-strategy-setup} and the convention following it), and $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\hat\\\\\\\\psi}$ denotes the expanded state on the six registers $\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}'\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}''\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}'\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}''$, obtained by adjoining to each player the two halves of $M$ pairs $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\mathrm{EPR}_q}$ (Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:expanded-state}). Unless stated otherwise, every $\\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\delta$ and $\\\\\\\\simeq_\\\\\\\\delta$ statement below is taken with respect to the state $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\hat\\\\\\\\psi}$, over the question distribution named in the statement, with register placements indicated by subscripts; ``all symmetric equivalents'' refers to the register interchanges of Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:symmetric-equivalents}.\\\\n    10\\\\t\\\\n    11\\\\tWe use the following objects from the preceding chapters: the combined point projectors $\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ acting on the expanded space (Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}); the projective polynomial-pair measurement $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\\\\\\\}$ of Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, with outcomes pairs of polynomials in $\\\\\\\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\F_q)$ and error function\\\\n    12\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n    13\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\delta_G(\\\\\\\\eps,m,d,q) = a(md)^a\\\\\\\\bigl(\\\\\\\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd}\\\\\\\\bigr)\\\\n    14\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n    15\\\\tfor universal constants $a > 1$, $0 < b < 1$. This is the error denoted $\\\\\\\\delta_S$ in Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}; we call it $\\\\\\\\delta_G$ here to distinguish the global polynomial-pair witness error from the enlarged error used below. We also use the decoding map $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec} = \\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}\\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\F_q) \\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\F_q^{M}$ of the low-degree code (Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:decoding-map}, applied with $H = \\\\\\\\F_q$; see Remark~\\\\\\\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}), which maps a polynomial $g$ to the string of its evaluations on the hypercube $\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m$; and the low-degree encoding $h \\\\\\\\mapsto g_h$ together with the vectors $\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^M$, characterized by $h \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_h(u)$ for all $u \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m$ \\\\\\\\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}. Both maps are $\\\\\\\\F_q$-linear, and $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g_h) = h$ for every $h \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^{M}$. We call a polynomial $g \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\F_q)$ an \\\\\\\\emph{encoding} if $g = g_h$ for some $h \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^{M}$ or, equivalently, if $g$ is multilinear; since a multilinear polynomial is determined by its values on $\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m$, the encodings are exactly the $g$ with $g_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g)} = g$ as polynomial representatives, and\\\\n    16\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n    17\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g(u)\\\\n    18\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\qquad \\\\\\\\text{for every encoding $g$ and every } u \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m\\\\\\\\;.\\\\n    19\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n    20\\\\tFor a $g$ that is not an encoding the two sides of this identity are the evaluations of the distinct polynomial representatives $g_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g)}$ and $g$; when $d \\\\\\\\leq q-1$ they consequently disagree at some $u$, but when $d \\\\\\\\geq q$ the two representatives may induce the same function on $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$, so pointwise validity of the identity does not characterize the encodings; see Remark~\\\\\\\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}.\\\\n    21\\\\t\\\\n    22\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Full-field decoding on bounded polynomials]\\\\\\\\label{def:qld-full-field-decoder}\\\\n    23\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.evalPoly, MIPStarRE.QPBT.decodeOnUniv, MIPStarRE.QPBT.decodeFq}\\\\n    24\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.encodingPoly, MIPStarRE.QPBT.IsEncoding}\\\\n    25\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\leanok\\\\n    26\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:decoding-map, def:low-degree-encoding}\\\\n    27\\\\t\\\\tFor a bounded polynomial representative $g$, let $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(g)$ be its evaluations on the Boolean cube, with every value of $\\\\\\\\F_q$ retained. For $h \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^M$, let $g_h$ denote its multilinear encoding. The predicate $\\\\\\\\mathrm{IsEncoding}(g)$ asserts the equality $g_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(g)}=g$ of polynomial representatives.\\\\n    28\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\n    29\\\\t\\\\n    30\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Linearity and inversion of the full-field decoder]\\\\\\\\label{lem:qld-decoder-linearity}\\\\n    31\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.decodeFq_add, MIPStarRE.QPBT.decodeFq_smul, MIPStarRE.QPBT.decodeFq_lowDegreeEncoding}\\\\n    32\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\leanok\\\\n    33\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:qld-full-field-decoder}\\\\n    34\\\\t\\\\tFor bounded polynomial representatives $g,g'$ and $c\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\F_q$, one has\\\\n    35\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n    36\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(g+g')=\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(g)+\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(g'),\\\\n    37\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\qquad\\\\n    38\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(cg)=c\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(g),\\\\n    39\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n    40\\\\t\\\\tand $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(g_h)=h$ for every $h\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\F_q^M$.\\\\n    41\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\n    42\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\n    43\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\leanok\\\\n    44\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:qld-full-field-decoder, def:low-degree-encoding}\\\\n    45\\\\t\\\\tThe decoder is given coordinatewise by evaluation of the representative at the points of the hypercube, and evaluation at a fixed point is $\\\\\\\\F_q$-linear in the representative, which gives the two displayed identities. For the third, the multilinear encoding $g_h$ takes the value $h_y$ at each point $y$ of the hypercube, because the indicator polynomials of \\\\\\\\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition} vanish at every other such point; hence the vector of its evaluations is $h$.\\\\n    46\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n    47\\\\t\\\\n    48\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[Evaluation identity for encoding outcomes]\\\\\\\\label{lem:qld-decoder-evaluation}\\\\n    49\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.decodeFq_dotProduct_indicatorVec}\\\\n    50\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\leanok\\\\n    51\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:qld-full-field-decoder, def:indicator-vector}\\\\n    52\\\\t\\\\tIf $g$ is an encoding, then\\\\n    53\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n    54\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(g)\\\\\\\\cdot\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)=g(u)\\\\n    55\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n    56\\\\t\\\\tfor every $u\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\F_q^m$.\\\\n    57\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\n    58\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\n    59\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\leanok\\\\n    60\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:qld-full-field-decoder, def:indicator-vector, def:low-degree-encoding}\\\\n    61\\\\t\\\\tBy \\\\\\\\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition} the left-hand side is the value at $u$ of the multilinear encoding $g_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}(g)}$. Since $g$ is an encoding, that representative equals $g$, so the value is $g(u)$.\\\\n    62\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n    63\\\\t\\\\n    64\\\\t% Local correction of the source (lines 1419 and 1492 of\\\\n    65\\\\t% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex): the source invokes its boolean\\\\n    66\\\\t% decoding map but describes it as returning all hypercube evaluations, and\\\\n    67\\\\t% asserts the decoder--encoder identity for every g of individual degree at\\\\n    68\\\\t% most d. The remark below records the corrected statements.\\\\n    69\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[Choice of decoding map and the decoder--encoder identity]\\\\\\\\label{rem:qld-decoding-identity}\\\\n    70\\\\t\\\\tThe source states the constructions of this chapter in terms of its boolean decoding map (the case $H = \\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}$ of Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:decoding-map}) while describing that map as returning the string of \\\\\\\\emph{all} evaluations of $g$ on the hypercube. The proofs require the latter reading, $H = \\\\\\\\F_q$, which is adopted throughout this chapter: the linearity of $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}$ and the identity $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g_h) = h$ for all $h \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^{M}$, both of which fail for the boolean map, are used in the symmetry step of the proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}. The source further asserts the identity $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g(u)$ for every $g \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\F_q)$. Since the left-hand side equals $g_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g)}(u)$, the identity, taken for all $u \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m$, states that the multilinear representative $g_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g)}$ and $g$ induce the same function on $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$. Every encoding satisfies it, because then $g_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g)} = g$ as polynomial representatives; a polynomial that is not an encoding need not: for $m = 1$, $d \\\\\\\\geq 2$, and $q > 2$, the polynomial $g(x) = x^2$ has $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_1(u) = u$, which differs from $g(u) = u^2$ at every $u \\\\\\\\notin \\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}$. When $d \\\\\\\\leq q-1$ the failure at some $u$ characterizes the polynomials that are not encodings, since evaluation is injective on representatives of individual degree at most $q-1$ (Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:polynomials-degree}); when $d \\\\\\\\geq q$ it does not: for the admissible tuple $(q,m,d) = (8,1,8)$, the polynomial $g(x) = x^8$ is not an encoding, yet $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_1(u) = u = u^8 = g(u)$ for every $u \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_8$. Accordingly, the proofs below invoke the identity only for encodings; in the proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} the contribution of the remaining outcomes is controlled by the bound \\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} on the mass that the measurement of Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} places on polynomials that are not encodings, while in the conjugation identity \\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the quantity $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly.\\\\n    71\\\\t\\\\tThe discrepancy and these two corrections are documented in\\\\n    72\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\cite{gap:qpbt_decoding-identity}.\\\\n    73\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{remark}\\\\n    74\\\\t\\\\n    75\\\\t\\\\\\\\section{The pulled-apart measurements and observables}\\\\n    76\\\\t\\\\n    77\\\\tThe measurement $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\\\\\\\}$ reads out an $X$-polynomial and a $Z$-polynomial simultaneously. We now separate the two readings again, this time as commuting actions on distinct registers: the polynomial outcome is measured on registers $\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}'$ (resp.\\\\\\\\ $\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}'$), while a genuine Pauli measurement acts on the EPR register $\\\\\\\\mathrm{A}''$ (resp.\\\\\\\\ $\\\\\\\\mathrm{B}''$).\\\\n    78\\\\t\\\\n    79\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Single-basis marginals of the polynomial-pair measurement]\\\\\\\\label{def:s-w-marginals}\\\\n    80\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.GlobalPairWitness.marginalPoly}\\\\n    81\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\leanok\\\\n    82\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{lem:qld-4-7}\\\\n     1\\\\timport MIPStarRE.QPBT.Algebra.LowDegreeCodeTheorems\\\\n     2\\\\timport MIPStarRE.QPBT.Combining.Defs\\\\n     3\\\\t\\\\n     4\\\\t/-! # Polynomial decoding for Pauli extraction\\\\n     5\\\\t\\\\n     6\\\\tThis module provides the Chapter 16 decoder on the Chapter 15 bounded\\\\n     7\\\\tpolynomial representative type.  The generic retained-value decoder and its\\\\n     8\\\\tspecialization to polynomial representatives live in\\\\n     9\\\\t`Algebra/LowDegreeCodeTheorems`; this module states the corresponding maps on\\\\n    10\\\\t`Poly P` used in the extraction chapter.\\\\n    11\\\\t\\\\n    12\\\\t## References\\\\n    13\\\\t\\\\n    14\\\\tThe decoder is `def:decoding-map`, blueprint\\\\n    15\\\\t`blueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:446-457`, with the extraction use\\\\n    16\\\\tat `blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:11-20` and paper\\\\n    17\\\\t`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1419-1450`.\\\\n    18\\\\tThe restricted decoder identity and its encoding hypothesis are documented in\\\\n    19\\\\t`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_decoding-identity.tex:87-123`.\\\\n    20\\\\t-/\\\\n    21\\\\t\\\\n    22\\\\topen scoped BigOperators\\\\n    23\\\\t\\\\n    24\\\\tnamespace MIPStarRE.QPBT\\\\n    25\\\\t\\\\n    26\\\\topen MIPStarRE.LDT MIPStarRE.LDT.Preliminaries\\\\n    27\\\\t\\\\n    28\\\\t/-! ## Representative evaluation and decoding -/\\\\n    29\\\\t\\\\n    30\\\\t/-- Evaluate the polynomial representative carried by `Poly P`.\\\\n    31\\\\t\\\\n    32\\\\tThis is polynomial evaluation on the source's `\\\\\\\\ideg_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\F_q)` carrier.  It\\\\n    33\\\\tdoes not identify representatives that induce the same polynomial function. -/\\\\n    34\\\\tnoncomputable def evalPoly {P : AdmissibleParams} (g : Poly P)\\\\n    35\\\\t    (x : Fin P.m → PauliScalar P) : PauliScalar P :=\\\\n    36\\\\t  MvPolynomial.eval x g.1\\\\n    37\\\\t\\\\n    38\\\\t/-- Decode a bounded polynomial while retaining only values in `H`.\\\\n    39\\\\t\\\\n    40\\\\tThis is the `H`-parameterized form of `def:decoding-map`; `decodeAt` remains\\\\n    41\\\\tthe decoding map for arbitrary functions.  The Boolean cube is embedded in\\\\n    42\\\\t`PauliScalar P` by `cubeEmbed`. -/\\\\n    43\\\\tnoncomputable def decodeOn {P : AdmissibleParams}\\\\n    44\\\\t    (H : Finset (PauliScalar P)) (g : Poly P) : PauliRegister P :=\\\\n    45\\\\t  decodeAt H (fun x => evalPoly g x)\\\\n    46\\\\t\\\\n    47\\\\t/-- Full-field specialization of `decodeOn` used in Chapter 16.\\\\n    48\\\\t\\\\n    49\\\\tThe retained-value set is `Finset.univ`, so every field value is kept.  This\\\\n    50\\\\tchoice is the correction recorded in\\\\n    51\\\\t`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_decoding-identity.tex:87-123`. -/\\\\n    52\\\\tnoncomputable abbrev decodeOnUniv {P : AdmissibleParams} (g : Poly P) :\\\\n    53\\\\t    PauliRegister P :=\\\\n    54\\\\t  decodeOn (Finset.univ : Finset (PauliScalar P)) g\\\\n    55\\\\t\\\\n    56\\\\t/-- The full-field decoder `\\\\\\\\operatorname{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\F_q}` on `Poly P`.\\\\n    57\\\\t\\\\n    58\\\\tThe map `decodeFqRep` has the same definition on arbitrary polynomial\\\\n    59\\\\trepresentatives; `decodeFq` restricts its domain to the bounded-degree class\\\\n    60\\\\t`Poly P`. -/\\\\n    61\\\\tnoncomputable abbrev decodeFq {P : AdmissibleParams} (g : Poly P) :\\\\n    62\\\\t    PauliRegister P :=\\\\n    63\\\\t  decodeOnUniv g\\\\n    64\\\\t\\\\n    65\\\\t/-! ## The encoding image -/\\\\n    66\\\\t\\\\n    67\\\\t/-- Membership of a multilinear encoding in the bounded representative class.\\\\n    68\\\\t\\\\n    69\\\\tEach indicator factor has degree zero away from its own variable and degree at\\\\n    70\\\\tmost one in that variable, so the multilinear encoding has individual degree at\\\\n    71\\\\tmost one, and hence at most `P.d` using `P.hd`.  This is the degree\\\\n    72\\\\tside-condition implicit in the source's `g_h` construction, not an extra\\\\n    73\\\\thypothesis of the decoding identity.  See `def:low-degree-encoding`, blueprint\\\\n    74\\\\t`blueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:404-424`, and paper\\\\n    75\\\\t`references/qpbt-paper/04_preliminaries.tex:832-897`. -/\\\\n    76\\\\ttheorem lowDegreeEncoding_mem_poly {P : AdmissibleParams}\\\\n    77\\\\t    (h : PauliRegister P) :\\\\n    78\\\\t    lowDegreeEncoding h ∈\\\\n    79\\\\t      MIPStarRE.LDT.Preliminaries.polyFunc P.m (PauliScalar P) P.d := by\\\\n    80\\\\t  rw [MvPolynomial.mem_restrictDegree]\\\\n    81\\\\t  intro s hs i\\\\n    82\\\\t  apply (MvPolynomial.monomial_le_degreeOf i hs).trans\\\\n    83\\\\t  rw [lowDegreeEncoding]\\\\n    84\\\\t  refine (MvPolynomial.degreeOf_sum_le i Finset.univ _).trans ?_\\\\n    85\\\\t  refine Finset.sup_le fun y _ => ?_\\\\n    86\\\\t  refine (show MvPolynomial.degreeOf i\\\\n    87\\\\t      (h y • indicatorPoly (K := PauliScalar P) y) ≤\\\\n    88\\\\t      MvPolynomial.degreeOf i (indicatorPoly (K := PauliScalar P) y) from ?_).trans ?_\\\\n    89\\\\t  · simpa only [MvPolynomial.smul_eq_C_mul] using\\\\n    90\\\\t      MvPolynomial.degreeOf_C_mul_le (indicatorPoly (K := PauliScalar P) y) i (h y)\\\\n    91\\\\t  rw [indicatorPoly]\\\\n    92\\\\t  refine (MvPolynomial.degreeOf_prod_le i Finset.univ _).trans ?_\\\\n    93\\\\t  calc\\\\n    94\\\\t    ∑ j, MvPolynomial.degreeOf i\\\\n    95\\\\t        (if y j then\\\\n    96\\\\t          (MvPolynomial.X j : MvPolynomial (Fin P.m) (PauliScalar P))\\\\n    97\\\\t        else 1 - MvPolynomial.X j) ≤\\\\n    98\\\\t        ∑ j, if i = j then 1 else 0 := by\\\\n    99\\\\t      apply Finset.sum_le_sum\\\\n   100\\\\t      intro j _\\\\n   101\\\\t      split\\\\n   102\\\\t      · exact le_of_eq (MvPolynomial.degreeOf_X i j)\\\\n   103\\\\t      · exact (MvPolynomial.degreeOf_sub_le i 1 (MvPolynomial.X j)).trans_eq (by\\\\n   104\\\\t          simp [MvPolynomial.degreeOf_X])\\\\n   105\\\\t    _ = 1 := by simp\\\\n   106\\\\t    _ ≤ P.d := P.hd\\\\n   107\\\\t\\\\n   108\\\\t/-- The multilinear encoding as a bounded polynomial representative.\\\\n   109\\\\t\\\\n   110\\\\tThe individual-degree bound is supplied by `lowDegreeEncoding_mem_poly`. -/\\\\n   111\\\\tnoncomputable def encodingPoly {P : AdmissibleParams} (h : PauliRegister P) :\\\\n   112\\\\t    Poly P :=\\\\n   113\\\\t  ⟨lowDegreeEncoding h, lowDegreeEncoding_mem_poly h⟩\\\\n   114\\\\t\\\\n   115\\\\t/-- A `Poly P` outcome is an encoding when its decoder reconstructs the same\\\\n   116\\\\tunderlying polynomial representative.\\\\n   117\\\\t\\\\n   118\\\\tThis is the explicit support restriction required by the corrected Chapter 16\\\\n   119\\\\tdecoding argument.  It is the `Poly` view of `IsEncodingRep`; no unrestricted\\\\n   120\\\\tdecoder/evaluation identity is asserted.  See\\\\n   121\\\\t`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_decoding-identity.tex:87-123`. -/\\\\n   122\\\\tdef IsEncoding {P : AdmissibleParams} (g : Poly P) : Prop :=\\\\n   123\\\\t  lowDegreeEncoding (decodeFq g) = g.1\\\\n   124\\\\t\\\\n   125\\\\t/-! ## Decoder companions -/\\\\n   126\\\\t\\\\n   127\\\\t/-- Linearity of the full-field decoder on bounded representatives.\\\\n   128\\\\t\\\\n   129\\\\tThis is the additive linearity of `lem:qld-decoder-linearity`, blueprint\\\\n   130\\\\t`ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:30-40`, used in the symmetry step of the source\\\\n   131\\\\targument at\\\\n   132\\\\t`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1546-1550`\\\\n   133\\\\tand blueprint `ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:239-244`.  It does not assert\\\\n   134\\\\tevaluation equality for non-encoding representatives.  See\\\\n   135\\\\t`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_decoding-identity.tex:87-123`. -/\\\\n   136\\\\ttheorem decodeFq_add {P : AdmissibleParams} (g h : Poly P) :\\\\n   137\\\\t    decodeFq (g + h) = decodeFq g + decodeFq h := by\\\\n   138\\\\t  funext y\\\\n   139\\\\t  simp [decodeOn, decodeAt, evalPoly, MvPolynomial.eval_add]\\\\n   140\\\\t\\\\n   141\\\\t/-- Scalar linearity of the full-field decoder on bounded representatives.\\\\n   142\\\\t\\\\n   143\\\\tThis companion has the same source scope as `decodeFq_add` and does not\\\\n   144\\\\tstrengthen the restricted decoder identity.  See\\\\n   145\\\\t`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_decoding-identity.tex:87-123`. -/\\\\n   146\\\\ttheorem decodeFq_smul {P : AdmissibleParams} (c : PauliScalar P) (g : Poly P) :\\\\n   147\\\\t    decodeFq (c • g) = c • decodeFq g := by\\\\n   148\\\\t  funext y\\\\n   149\\\\t  simp [decodeOn, decodeAt, evalPoly, MvPolynomial.smul_eval]\\\\n   150\\\\t\\\\n   151\\\\t/-- The full-field decoder is a left inverse to `encodingPoly`.\\\\n   152\\\\t\\\\n   153\\\\tThis is the identity `\\\\\\\\operatorname{Dec}(g_h)=h` of\\\\n   154\\\\t`lem:qld-decoder-linearity`, blueprint `ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:30-40`, for\\\\n   155\\\\tthe full-field decoder specified at\\\\n   156\\\\t`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1419-1420`.\\\\n   157\\\\tThe degree proof is separated into `lowDegreeEncoding_mem_poly`; no additional\\\\n   158\\\\tencoding or interpolation hypothesis is introduced.  See\\\\n   159\\\\t`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_decoding-identity.tex:87-123`. -/\\\\n   160\\\\ttheorem decodeFq_lowDegreeEncoding {P : AdmissibleParams}\\\\n   161\\\\t    (h : PauliRegister P) :\\\\n   162\\\\t    decodeFq (encodingPoly h) = h := by\\\\n   163\\\\t  funext y\\\\n   164\\\\t  simpa only [decodeFq, decodeOnUniv, decodeOn, decodeAt, evalPoly,\\\\n   165\\\\t    encodingPoly, lowDegreeEnc, Finset.mem_univ, if_true] using\\\\n   166\\\\t      congrFun\\\\n   167\\\\t        (decodeAt_lowDegreeEnc\\\\n   168\\\\t          (Finset.univ : Finset (PauliScalar P)) h\\\\n   169\\\\t            (fun z => Finset.mem_univ (h z))) y\\\\n   170\\\\t\\\\n   171\\\\t/-- Decoder/evaluation agreement for an encoding representative only.\\\\n   172\\\\t\\\\n   173\\\\tThe hypothesis `hg : IsEncoding g` is mandatory.  For a general bounded\\\\n   174\\\\tpolynomial, Boolean-cube values determine its multilinear interpolant, which\\\\n   175\\\\tneed not equal the original representative.  This is the corrected form of\\\\n   176\\\\tthe source step at\\\\n   177\\\\t`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1483-1492`;\\\\n   178\\\\tsee `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_decoding-identity.tex:87-123`.\\\\n   179\\\\t\\\\n   180\\\\t**Local fix:** The source's unrestricted identity is false, as witnessed by\\\\n   181\\\\t`g(x) = x^2` over a field with more than two elements.  The encoding restriction\\\\n   182\\\\tand representative semantics are documented in\\\\n   183\\\\t`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_decoding-identity.tex:87-123`. -/\\\\n   184\\\\ttheorem decodeFq_dotProduct_indicatorVec {P : AdmissibleParams}\\\\n   185\\\\t    {g : Poly P} (hg : IsEncoding g) (x : Fin P.m → PauliScalar P) :\\\\n   186\\\\t    dotProduct (decodeFq g) (indicatorVec x) = evalPoly g x := by\\\\n   187\\\\t  unfold IsEncoding at hg\\\\n   188\\\\t  calc\\\\n   189\\\\t    dotProduct (decodeFq g) (indicatorVec x) = lowDegreeEnc (decodeFq g) x :=\\\\n   190\\\\t      (lowDegreeEnc_eq_dotProduct (decodeFq g) x).symm\\\\n   191\\\\t    _ = evalPoly g x := by simp [lowDegreeEnc, evalPoly, hg]\\\\n   192\\\\t\\\\n   193\\\\tend MIPStarRE.QPBT\\\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Algebra/Decoding.lean:167:        (decodeAt_lowDegreeEnc\\\\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex:446