\t\\\\tindividual-degree form.\\\\n   398\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{remark}\\\\n   399\\\\t\\\\n   400\\\\tThe Schwartz--Zippel lemma implies that low-degree polynomials form an\\\\n   401\\\\terror-correcting code with good distance, the \\\\\\\\emph{low-degree code} (a special\\\\n   402\\\\tcase of the generalized Reed--Muller code).\\\\n   403\\\\t\\\\n   404\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Low-degree encoding]\\\\\\\\label{def:low-degree-encoding}\\\\n   405\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:polynomials-degree}\\\\n   406\\\\t\\\\tFix an integer $m \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\N$ and let $M = 2^m$. For every $y \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m$\\\\n   407\\\\t\\\\tdefine the $m$-variate multilinear polynomial over $\\\\\\\\F_q$\\\\n   408\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n   409\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}(x)\\\\n   410\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t= \\\\\\\\prod_{i : y_i = 1} x_i \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\prod_{i : y_i = 0} (1 - x_i)\\\\\\\\;,\\\\n   411\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n   412\\\\t\\\\tidentifying $\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}$ with a subset of $\\\\\\\\F_q$. Restricted to the subcube\\\\n   413\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m$, the polynomial $\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}$ vanishes everywhere except\\\\n   414\\\\t\\\\tat $x = y$, where it equals $1$. For $a \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^M$, label the coordinates\\\\n   415\\\\t\\\\tof $a$ as $a_y$ for $y \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m$ (identifying $\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m$ with\\\\n   416\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\{1, \\\\\\\\dots, M\\\\\\\\}$ by the lexicographic ordering). The \\\\\\\\emph{low-degree\\\\n   417\\\\t\\\\tencoding of $a$} is the multilinear polynomial $g_a : \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\F_q$,\\\\n   418\\\\t\\\\t% Paper equation label kept on the display it names.\\\\n   419\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\label{eq:ld-encoding}\\\\n   420\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tg_a(x) = \\\\\\\\sum_{y \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m} a_y \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}(x)\\\\\\\\;.\\\\n   421\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\n   422\\\\t\\\\tFor every $y \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m$ one has $g_a(y) = a_y$, and $g_a$ has total\\\\n   423\\\\t\\\\tdegree at most $m$.\\\\n   424\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\n   425\\\\t\\\\n   426\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Indicator vector and linearity of the encoding]\\\\\\\\label{def:indicator-vector}\\\\n   427\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:low-degree-encoding, def:dot-product-orthogonal}\\\\n   428\\\\t\\\\tFor $x \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m$, the \\\\\\\\emph{indicator vector} is\\\\n   429\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n   430\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(x)\\\\n   431\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t= \\\\\\\\bigl(\\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}(x)\\\\\\\\bigr)_{y \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m} \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^M\\\\\\\\;.\\\\n   432\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n   433\\\\t\\\\tWith this notation the low-degree encoding satisfies, for every\\\\n   434\\\\t\\\\t$a \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^M$ and $x \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m$,\\\\n   435\\\\t\\\\t% Paper equation label kept on the display it names.\\\\n   436\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\label{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}\\\\n   437\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tg_a(x) = a \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\mathrm{ind}_m(x)\\\\\\\\;;\\\\n   438\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\n   439\\\\t\\\\tin particular the map $a \\\\\\\\mapsto g_a$ is $\\\\\\\\F_q$-linear.\\\\n   440\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\n   441\\\\t\\\\n   442\\\\tSince encodings $g_a$ are multilinear, hence of total degree at most $m$, two\\\\n   443\\\\tencodings of distinct strings agree on at most an $m/q$ fraction of points of\\\\n   444\\\\t$\\\\\\\\F_q^m$ by Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel}.\\\\n   445\\\\t\\\\n   446\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Decoding maps]\\\\\\\\label{def:decoding-map}\\\\n   447\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.decodeAt, MIPStarRE.QPBT.decodeBool, MIPStarRE.QPBT.decodeAt_lowDegreeEnc}\\\\n   448\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\leanok\\\\n   449\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:low-degree-encoding}\\\\n   450\\\\t\\\\tFor $H \\\\\\\\subseteq \\\\\\\\F_q$, the \\\\\\\\emph{decoding map} $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_H$ takes as\\\\n   451\\\\t\\\\tinput a polynomial $g : \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\F_q$ and returns the vector\\\\n   452\\\\t\\\\t$a \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^M$ defined by: for all $y \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}^m$, set $a_y = g(y)$ if\\\\n   453\\\\t\\\\t$g(y) \\\\\\\\in H$, and $a_y = 0$ otherwise. For all $a \\\\\\\\in H^M$ one has\\\\n   454\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_H(g_a) = a$. We write $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}(\\\\\\\\cdot)$ for the boolean\\\\n   455\\\\t\\\\tdecoding map $\\\\\\\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}}(\\\\\\\\cdot)$.\\\\n   456\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\n   457\\\\t\\\\n   458\\\\t\\\\\\\\section{Lines}\\\\n   459\\\\t\\\\n   460\\\\tThe question distributions of the classical low individual degree game and of\\\\n   461\\\\tthe Pauli basis test (Chapter~\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-test}) sample lines in $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$;\\\\n   462\\\\twe record here the definition of lines and their canonical representatives.\\\\n   463\\\\t\\\\n   464\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Lines]\\\\\\\\label{def:line}\\\\n   465\\\\t\\\\tA \\\\\\\\emph{line} $\\\\\\\\ell$ in $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$ specified by a pair\\\\n   466\\\\t\\\\t$(u,v) \\\\\\\\in (\\\\\\\\F_q^m)^2$ is the subset\\\\n   467\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\\\\\label{eq:line-set}\\\\n   468\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\ell(u,v) = \\\\\\\\bigl\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\, u + tv : t \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\bigr\\\\\\\\} \\\\\\\\subseteq \\\\\\\\F_q^m\\\\\\\\;.\\\\n   469\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\n   470\\\\t\\\\tThe vector $v$ is called a \\\\\\\\emph{direction} of $\\\\\\\\ell(u,v)$; the case $v = 0$\\\\n404:\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Low-degree encoding]\\\\\\\\label{def:low-degree-encoding}\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\nresults/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr184-20260905-04.jsonl-350-{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_179\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"comman